::MCR_U06_L04_Quiz_Q01::
Question ID: MCR_U06_L04_Quiz_Q01
MLA Standard: Support-only extension of MLA.MCR.GM.4 through MLA.MCR.GM.6 and MLA.MCR.QR.2
Question:
An object has mass 80 grams and volume 20 cubic cm. What is its density?
{
=A. 4 grams per cubic cm#Correct. Density = 80 divided by 20 = 4 grams per cubic cm.
~B. 100 grams per cubic cm#This adds mass and volume. Density uses division.
~C. 1600 grams per cubic cm#This multiplies mass and volume. Density is mass divided by volume.
~D. 60 grams per cubic cm#This subtracts volume from mass. Use the density formula.
}


::MCR_U06_L04_Quiz_Q02::
Question ID: MCR_U06_L04_Quiz_Q02
MLA Standard: Support-only extension of MLA.MCR.GM.4 through MLA.MCR.GM.6 and MLA.MCR.QR.2
Question:
A material has density 7 grams per cubic cm and volume 9 cubic cm. What is its mass?
{
~A. 16 grams#This adds density and volume. Mass is density times volume.
~B. 45 grams#This does not use density times volume. Multiply 7 by 9 to find mass.
=C. 63 grams#Correct. Multiply density by volume to find mass.
~D. 1.29 grams#This divides volume by density. Use multiplication for mass.
}


::MCR_U06_L04_Quiz_Q03::
Question ID: MCR_U06_L04_Quiz_Q03
MLA Standard: Support-only extension of MLA.MCR.GM.4 through MLA.MCR.GM.6 and MLA.MCR.QR.2
Question:
A sample has mass 54 grams and density 6 grams per cubic cm. What is the volume?
{
~A. 60 cubic cm#This adds mass and density. Volume requires division.
=B. 9 cubic cm#Correct. Volume = 54 divided by 6 = 9 cubic cm.
~C. 324 cubic cm#This multiplies mass and density. Volume is mass divided by density.
~D. 48 cubic cm#This subtracts density from mass. That is not the density relationship.
}


::MCR_U06_L04_Quiz_Q04::
Question ID: MCR_U06_L04_Quiz_Q04
MLA Standard: Support-only extension of MLA.MCR.GM.4 through MLA.MCR.GM.6 and MLA.MCR.QR.2
Question:
Which unit is a density unit?
{
~A. square feet#Square feet measure area, not density.
~B. cubic inches#Cubic inches measure volume only.
~C. grams#Grams measure mass only.
=D. grams per cubic cm#Correct. Density compares mass to volume.
}


::MCR_U06_L04_Quiz_Q05::
Question ID: MCR_U06_L04_Quiz_Q05
MLA Standard: Support-only extension of MLA.MCR.GM.4 through MLA.MCR.GM.6 and MLA.MCR.QR.2
Question:
What does density compare?
{
~A. length and width#Length and width are used for area, not density.
=B. mass and volume#Correct. Density compares mass to volume.
~C. time and distance#Time and distance form speed, not density.
~D. cost and quantity#Cost per quantity is a rate, but not density.
}


::MCR_U06_L04_Quiz_Q06::
Question ID: MCR_U06_L04_Quiz_Q06
MLA Standard: Support-only extension of MLA.MCR.GM.4 through MLA.MCR.GM.6 and MLA.MCR.QR.2
Question:
An object has mass 120 grams and volume 15 cubic cm. What is its density?
{
=A. 8 grams per cubic cm#Correct. Density = 120 divided by 15 = 8 grams per cubic cm.
~B. 135 grams per cubic cm#This adds mass and volume. Use division for density.
~C. 1800 grams per cubic cm#This multiplies mass and volume. Density is a ratio.
~D. 105 grams per cubic cm#This subtracts volume from mass. Use mass divided by volume.
}


::MCR_U06_L04_Quiz_Q07::
Question ID: MCR_U06_L04_Quiz_Q07
MLA Standard: Support-only extension of MLA.MCR.GM.4 through MLA.MCR.GM.6 and MLA.MCR.QR.2
Question:
A material has density 5 grams per cubic cm and volume 14 cubic cm. What is the mass?
{
~A. 19 grams#This adds density and volume. Mass is density times volume.
~B. 9 grams#This subtracts density from volume. That does not find mass.
~C. 2.8 grams#This divides volume by density. Use multiplication for mass.
=D. 70 grams#Correct. Mass = 5 x 14 = 70 grams.
}


::MCR_U06_L04_Quiz_Q08::
Question ID: MCR_U06_L04_Quiz_Q08
MLA Standard: Support-only extension of MLA.MCR.GM.4 through MLA.MCR.GM.6 and MLA.MCR.QR.2
Question:
If density = mass / volume, what is mass equal to?
{
~A. density / volume#This divides density by volume, which does not produce mass.
~B. volume / density#This reverses the relationship. Mass requires multiplication.
=C. density x volume#Correct. Rearranging density = mass / volume gives mass = density x volume.
~D. density + volume#Adding does not preserve the ratio relationship.
}


::MCR_U06_L04_Quiz_Q09::
Question ID: MCR_U06_L04_Quiz_Q09
MLA Standard: Support-only extension of MLA.MCR.GM.4 through MLA.MCR.GM.6 and MLA.MCR.QR.2
Question:
A sample has mass 72 grams and density 8 grams per cubic cm. What is its volume?
{
=A. 9 cubic cm#Correct. Volume = 72 divided by 8 = 9 cubic cm.
~B. 80 cubic cm#This adds mass and density. Volume uses division.
~C. 576 cubic cm#This multiplies mass and density. Volume is mass divided by density.
~D. 64 cubic cm#This subtracts density from mass. That is not the correct relationship.
}


::MCR_U06_L04_Quiz_Q10::
Question ID: MCR_U06_L04_Quiz_Q10
MLA Standard: Support-only extension of MLA.MCR.GM.4 through MLA.MCR.GM.6 and MLA.MCR.QR.2
Question:
Which interpretation matches 3 grams per cubic cm?
{
~A. 3 cubic cm for each gram#This reverses the unit. The unit says grams for each cubic cm.
=B. 3 grams for each cubic cm#Correct. Grams per cubic cm means mass in each unit of volume.
~C. 3 square cm for each gram#Square cm measures area, not volume.
~D. 3 grams for each foot#The volume unit is cubic cm, not feet.
}


::MCR_U06_L04_Quiz_Q11::
Question ID: MCR_U06_L04_Quiz_Q11
MLA Standard: Support-only extension of MLA.MCR.GM.4 through MLA.MCR.GM.6 and MLA.MCR.QR.2
Question:
An object has mass 45 grams and volume 5 cubic cm. What is its density?
{
~A. 50 grams per cubic cm#This adds mass and volume. Density uses division.
~B. 225 grams per cubic cm#This multiplies mass and volume. Density is mass divided by volume.
=C. 9 grams per cubic cm#Correct. Density = 45 divided by 5 = 9 grams per cubic cm.
~D. 40 grams per cubic cm#This subtracts volume from mass. That does not find density.
}


::MCR_U06_L04_Quiz_Q12::
Question ID: MCR_U06_L04_Quiz_Q12
MLA Standard: Support-only extension of MLA.MCR.GM.4 through MLA.MCR.GM.6 and MLA.MCR.QR.2
Question:
A material has density 12 grams per cubic cm and volume 4 cubic cm. What is its mass?
{
=A. 48 grams#Correct. Mass = density x volume = 12 x 4 = 48 grams.
~B. 16 grams#This adds density and volume. Mass requires multiplication.
~C. 3 grams#This divides density by volume. That does not find total mass.
~D. 8 grams#This subtracts volume from density. That is not the measurement relationship.
}


::MCR_U06_L04_Quiz_Q13::
Question ID: MCR_U06_L04_Quiz_Q13
MLA Standard: Support-only extension of MLA.MCR.GM.4 through MLA.MCR.GM.6 and MLA.MCR.QR.2
Question:
Which formula finds volume when mass and density are known?
{
~A. density / mass#This reverses the relationship. Volume is mass divided by density.
~B. mass x density#This finds too large a value and does not isolate volume.
~C. mass + density#Adding does not undo the density ratio.
=D. mass / density#Correct. Volume = mass divided by density.
}


::MCR_U06_L04_Quiz_Q14::
Question ID: MCR_U06_L04_Quiz_Q14
MLA Standard: Support-only extension of MLA.MCR.GM.4 through MLA.MCR.GM.6 and MLA.MCR.QR.2
Question:
A sample has mass 96 grams and volume 12 cubic cm. What is its density?
{
~A. 108 grams per cubic cm#This adds mass and volume. Density uses division.
=B. 8 grams per cubic cm#Correct. Density = 96 divided by 12 = 8 grams per cubic cm.
~C. 1152 grams per cubic cm#This multiplies mass and volume. Density is not a product.
~D. 84 grams per cubic cm#This subtracts volume from mass. Use the density formula.
}


::MCR_U06_L04_Quiz_Q15::
Question ID: MCR_U06_L04_Quiz_Q15
MLA Standard: Support-only extension of MLA.MCR.GM.4 through MLA.MCR.GM.6 and MLA.MCR.QR.2
Question:
Why is density a ratio?
{
~A. It only uses one measurement#Density uses two measurements: mass and volume.
~B. It always uses square units#Density usually uses mass per volume, not square units.
=C. It compares mass to volume#Correct. A ratio compares two quantities, and density compares mass to volume.
~D. It measures distance around a shape#Distance around a shape is perimeter, not density.
}


::MCR_U06_L04_Quiz_Q16::
Question ID: MCR_U06_L04_Quiz_Q16
MLA Standard: Support-only extension of MLA.MCR.GM.4 through MLA.MCR.GM.6 and MLA.MCR.QR.2
Question:
A material has density 10 grams per cubic cm and volume 6 cubic cm. What is its mass?
{
~A. 16 grams#This adds density and volume. Mass is density times volume.
~B. 4 grams#This subtracts volume from density. That does not find mass.
~C. 1.67 grams#This divides volume by density. To find mass, multiply.
=D. 60 grams#Correct. Mass = 10 x 6 = 60 grams.
}


::MCR_U06_L04_Quiz_Q17::
Question ID: MCR_U06_L04_Quiz_Q17
MLA Standard: Support-only extension of MLA.MCR.GM.4 through MLA.MCR.GM.6 and MLA.MCR.QR.2
Question:
An object has mass 33 grams and volume 11 cubic cm. What is its density?
{
=A. 3 grams per cubic cm#Correct. Density = 33 divided by 11 = 3 grams per cubic cm.
~B. 44 grams per cubic cm#This adds mass and volume. Density requires division.
~C. 363 grams per cubic cm#This multiplies mass and volume. Density is mass divided by volume.
~D. 22 grams per cubic cm#This subtracts volume from mass. That is not density.
}


::MCR_U06_L04_Quiz_Q18::
Question ID: MCR_U06_L04_Quiz_Q18
MLA Standard: Support-only extension of MLA.MCR.GM.4 through MLA.MCR.GM.6 and MLA.MCR.QR.2
Question:
A sample has mass 100 grams and density 5 grams per cubic cm. What is its volume?
{
~A. 500 cubic cm#This multiplies mass and density. Volume is mass divided by density.
=B. 20 cubic cm#Correct. Volume = 100 divided by 5 = 20 cubic cm.
~C. 105 cubic cm#This adds mass and density. That does not solve for volume.
~D. 95 cubic cm#This subtracts density from mass. That is not the density relationship.
}


::MCR_U06_L04_Quiz_Q19::
Question ID: MCR_U06_L04_Quiz_Q19
MLA Standard: Support-only extension of MLA.MCR.GM.4 through MLA.MCR.GM.6 and MLA.MCR.QR.2
Question:
Which quantity is usually measured in grams in these density problems?
{
~A. volume#Volume is measured in cubic units such as cubic cm.
~B. density#Density uses a ratio unit such as grams per cubic cm.
~C. area#Area is measured in square units, not grams.
=D. mass#Correct. Mass is the quantity measured in grams.
}


::MCR_U06_L04_Quiz_Q20::
Question ID: MCR_U06_L04_Quiz_Q20
MLA Standard: Support-only extension of MLA.MCR.GM.4 through MLA.MCR.GM.6 and MLA.MCR.QR.2
Question:
An object has volume 7 cubic cm and density 9 grams per cubic cm. What is its mass?
{
~A. 16 grams#This adds volume and density. Mass is density times volume.
~B. 2 grams#This subtracts volume from density. That does not find mass.
=C. 63 grams#Correct. Mass = 9 x 7 = 63 grams.
~D. 1.29 grams#This divides 9 by 7. Use multiplication for mass.
}

